  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 116 PM EST Friday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Dry and seasonably mild weather will continue through \nmuch of the day on Sunday as high pressure slowly departs to the \neast. A coastal low pressure that is currently being watched by NHC \nwill quickly move up the east coast over the weekend. Physics based \nand AI models continue to flip flop slightly over the actual track \nof the coastal low on Sunday and how far inland the precipitation \nmay spread Sunday night and Monday. Based on the latest 12Z runs of \nthese models, we have increased rainfall amounts to 0.5-0.8 inches \nacross southeastern Vermont where the most rain is expected to just \na tenth of an inch at Saranac Lake. A strong gradient in rainfall \ntotals is expected regardless of the overall low track but it's \npossible we see slightly higher or even slightly lower rainfall \ntotals based on the overall low track. Nevertheless, this rainfall \nwill be extremely beneficial as we have seen drought conditions \ndevelop across the region over the past two months. Rainfall will \ntaper off during the morning hours on Monday with a strong cold \nfront quick to follow. Temperatures will plummet Monday night with \ntemperatures dropping into the 20s after midnight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Monday night into Tuesday, northwest winds will increase \nto 10-15 mph and gusts 25-30 mph as pressure gradients tighten. A \nsurface low pressure will be moving northeastward into the Canadian \nMaritimes while a strong surface high builds into the northeastern \nUnited States in its place. Tuesday morning looks to be quite brisk, \nwith lows in the upper teens and 20s across northern New York and \nVermont. Combined with the breezy northwest winds, this will result \nin widespread apparent temperatures in the teens and lower 20s \npersisting through much of the day Tuesday and Tuesday night. With \nthis cold air mass diving into the region so quickly after \nanticipated rainfall from the early week, it is possible for \nstanding water to freeze, leaving slippery patches of ice on \nuntreated surfaces. Tuesday night, temperatures will remain cold, \nbut we should see winds slacken a bit. Expect some slow moderation \nin temperatures each day of the latter half of the week, resulting \nin highs in the upper 30s to mid 40s by Thanksgiving, but these are \nstill on the cool side of seasonal normals this time of year. Lows \nwill be similarly chilly with widespread 20s F by Thanksgiving \nnight. Thanksgiving and/or the day after could be impacted by \nanother coastal low pressure, but model spread continues to be wide. \nIf we do see the coastal low track close enough to bring \nprecipitation to the region, it may be a wintry mix with the \npotential for gusty winds. However, some solutions keep us dry the \nentire late week. Time will tell, but we continue to watch this \nperiod closely in preparation for increased holiday travel.",
